{
  "concept": "Archimedes' spiral r = θ: the distance from the pole grows in direct proportion to the angle swept, so one full counterclockwise turn produces a single evenly widening open loop.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Polar grid: seven concentric gray circles of radii 1 through 7 centered at the origin",
    "Gray radial spokes every 45° (π/4) crossing the concentric circles",
    "Horizontal axis with arrowheads at both ends, integer ticks labeled -7 … -1, 1 … 7",
    "Vertical axis with arrowheads at both ends, integer ticks labeled -7 … -1, 1 … 7",
    "Solid dark navy curve: Archimedes' spiral r = θ for θ from 0 to 2π, starting at the origin and ending on the positive x-axis near x ≈ 6.28",
    "Filled black sample dot at (π/4, π/4) — just above and right of the origin",
    "Filled black sample dot at (π/2, π/2) — directly above the origin, height ≈ 1.57",
    "Filled black sample dot at (π, π) — on the negative x-axis, about 3.14 units left",
    "Filled black sample dot at (5π/4, 5π/4) — lower left, about 3.9 units out",
    "Filled black sample dot at (7π/4, 7π/4) — lower right, about 5.5 units out",
    "Filled black sample dot at (2π, 2π) — on the positive x-axis, about 6.28 units out",
    "Ordered-pair labels in stacked-fraction form beside each dot: (π/4, π/4), (π/2, π/2), (π, π), (5π/4, 5π/4), (7π/4, 7π/4), (2π, 2π)",
    "Moving tracer point on the curve with its radius segment from the origin (interactive layer)",
    "Readout panel showing current θ and current r = θ (interactive layer)",
    "Highlight overlay: a colored radial ray at the selected angle and a colored circle of the matching radius (interactive layer)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "thetaSweep",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "θ (radians)",
      "range": [0, 6.283, 0.01],
      "teaches": "As θ increases the tracer point moves counterclockwise and its distance from the origin increases by exactly the same amount — the radius readout always equals the angle readout, which is what r = θ means.",
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "traceSpiral",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "Sweep the radius and draw the spiral",
      "loop": true,
      "teaches": "The spiral is generated by two steady motions at once: the ray rotates at a constant rate while the point slides outward along that ray at a constant rate, so each successive turn would sit a fixed distance farther out.",
      "sample_note": "The rotating radius segment lengthens as it turns and leaves the navy curve behind it."
    },
    {
      "id": "samplePoint",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Clicking a labeled dot lights its angle ray and its matching radius circle together, showing why the two entries of each polar coordinate pair are the same number."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Start at the pole",
      "narration": "At θ = 0 the radius is also 0, so the curve begins exactly at the origin. The tracer sits on the pole with no radius segment yet.",
      "state": { "thetaSweep": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "A quarter turn out",
      "narration": "Drag θ to π/2. The tracer has rotated a quarter turn counterclockwise and stands about 1.57 units straight above the origin — the plotted dot labeled (π/2, π/2).",
      "state": { "thetaSweep": 1.571 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Half a turn — radius π",
      "narration": "At θ = π the point lies on the negative x-axis, about 3.14 units out. The label (π, π) reads angle first, radius second, and both are the same number.",
      "state": { "thetaSweep": 3.142 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Three quarters and beyond",
      "narration": "Past 5π/4 and 7π/4 the loop keeps widening at a steady rate; the gaps between successive gray circles crossed by the curve stay uniform.",
      "state": { "thetaSweep": 5.498 }
    },
    {
      "title": "One full turn closes the loop",
      "narration": "At θ = 2π the curve returns to the positive x-axis but now about 6.28 units out instead of at the origin — one full revolution has pushed the radius out by 2π.",
      "state": { "thetaSweep": 6.283 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Watch it drawn",
      "narration": "Play the animation and click any labeled dot: the rotating ray lengthens as it turns, and each highlighted ray plus its matching circle confirms r = θ at that point.",
      "state": { "thetaSweep": 6.283 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Every marked point has identical coordinates because the radius IS the angle — steady rotation paired with steady outward motion is exactly what makes an evenly widening spiral instead of a circle.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Square plot centered on the origin. Background: light-gray concentric circles at integer radii 1–7 and light-gray radial spokes every π/4. Black horizontal and vertical axes with arrowheads at all four ends, integer tick labels from -7 to 7 (labels sit just off the axis, tightly spaced near the origin as in the original). The spiral is a single thick dark navy (≈ #1F3F6E) curve from the origin sweeping counterclockwise through roughly (0.55, 0.55) at π/4, straight up at π/2, left at π ≈ 3.1, down-left at 5π/4, down-right at 7π/4, ending on the positive x-axis near 6.28 — it never crosses itself and its distance from center grows linearly. Six filled black dots with ordered-pair labels written as stacked fractions in parentheses, placed just outside the curve at each dot (upper-right for π/4 and π/2, upper-left for π, lower-left for 5π/4, lower-right for 7π/4, upper-right for 2π). Interactive layer: a bright accent color (e.g. orange) for the moving tracer dot, its radius segment from the origin, the highlighted angle ray, and the matching radius circle; a small corner readout showing θ and r as equal values in both radian-fraction and decimal form."
}

