        "thetaSweep": 5.498
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "One full turn closes the loop",
      "narration": "At θ = 2π the curve returns to the positive x-axis but now about 6.28 units out instead of at the origin — one full revolution has pushed the radius out by 2π.",
      "state": {
        "thetaSweep": 6.283
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Watch it drawn",
      "narration": "Play the animation and click any labeled dot: the rotating ray lengthens as it turns, and each highlighted ray plus its matching circle confirms r = θ at that point.",
      "state": {
        "thetaSweep": 6.283
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Every marked point has identical coordinates because the radius IS the angle — steady rotation paired with steady outward motion is exactly what makes an evenly widening spiral instead of a circle.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Square plot centered on the origin. Background: light-gray concentric circles at integer radii 1–7 and light-gray radial spokes every π/4. Black horizontal and vertical axes with arrowheads at all four ends, integer tick labels from -7 to 7 (labels sit just off the axis, tightly spaced near the origin as in the original). The spiral is a single thick dark navy (≈ #1F3F6E) curve from the origin sweeping counterclockwise through roughly (0.55, 0.55) at π/4, straight up at π/2, left at π ≈ 3.1, down-left at 5π/4, down-right at 7π/4, ending on the positive x-axis near 6.28 — it never crosses itself and its distance from center grows linearly. Six filled black dots with ordered-pair labels written as stacked fractions in parentheses, placed just outside the curve at each dot (upper-right for π/4 and π/2, upper-left for π, lower-left for 5π/4, lower-right for 7π/4, upper-right for 2π). Interactive layer: a bright accent color (e.g. orange) for the moving tracer dot, its radius segment from the origin, the highlighted angle ray, and the matching radius circle; a small corner readout showing θ and r as equal values in both radian-fraction and decimal form."
}

Here is the generated code to evaluate:

<!-- @FIGURE_UI_BEGIN -->
<strong style="font-size:12px">Archimedes' spiral &nbsp;r = θ</strong>
<p id="demoNarration" style="margin:0;font-size:11px;line-height:1.4;max-width:260px;color:#222"></p>
<span style="display:flex;gap:6px;align-items:center">
  <button onclick="prevStep()">◀</button>
  <span id="stepLabel" style="font-size:11px;min-width:34px;text-align:center">1/6</span>
  <button onclick="nextStep()">▶</button>
</span>
<label>θ (radians): <output id="thetaSweepVal">0.00</output>
  <input id="thetaSweepInput" type="range" min="0" max="6.283" step="0.01" value="0"
    oninput="document.getElementById('thetaSweepVal').value=(+this.value).toFixed(2);update_thetaSweep(+this.value)">
</label>
<button id="playBtn" onclick="trigger_traceSpiral()">▶ Sweep the radius</button>
<button onclick="trigger_reset()">↺ Reset to original figure</button>
<span style="font-size:10.5px;color:#555;line-height:1.35">Hover a labelled black dot for its values; click it to light up its angle ray and the matching radius circle.</span>
<span style="font-size:10.5px;color:#1F3F6E;line-height:1.35"><b>Key idea:</b> the radius <i>is</i> the angle, so both entries of every ordered pair are the same number.</span>
<!-- @FIGURE_UI_END -->
// @FIGURE_CODE_BEGIN
var TWO_PI = Math.PI * 2;
var NAVY = '#1F3F6E';
var ACCENT = '#E8792B';
var PAD = 12;

var spState = { theta: 0, interacted: false, playing: false, highlight: null };

function T(s) { return { t: 'x', s: s }; }
function F(n, d) { return { t: 'f', n: n, d: d }; }

var marks = [
  { th: Math.PI / 4, plain: 'π/4, π/4', toks: [T('('), F('π', '4'), T(', '), F('π', '4'), T(')')], lx: 0.80, ly: 1.05, align: 'left' },
  { th: Math.PI / 2, plain: 'π/2, π/2', toks: [T('('), F('π', '2'), T(', '), F('π', '2'), T(')')], lx: 0.30, ly: 2.62, align: 'left' },
  { th: Math.PI, plain: 'π, π', toks: [T('(π, π)')], lx: -4.40, ly: 1.00, align: 'center' },
  { th: 5 * Math.PI / 4, plain: '5π/4, 5π/4', toks: [T('('), F('5π', '4'), T(', '), F('5π', '4'), T(')')], lx: -5.25, ly: -2.95, align: 'center' },
  { th: 7 * Math.PI / 4, plain: '7π/4, 7π/4', toks: [T('('), F('7π', '4'), T(', '), F('7π', '4'), T(')')], lx: 5.45, ly: -4.65, align: 'center' },
  { th: TWO_PI, plain: '2π, 2π', toks: [T('(2π, 2π)')], lx: 5.05, ly: 1.05, align: 'center' }
];
marks.forEach(function (m) { m.px = m.th * Math.cos(m.th); m.py = m.th * Math.sin(m.th); });

var spiralPts = [];
for (var i = 0; i <= 480; i++) { var t = TWO_PI * i / 480; spiralPts.push({ x: t * Math.cos(t), y: t * Math.sin(t) }); }

var dotPts = [{ x: 0, y: 0 }].concat(marks.map(function (m) { return { x: m.px, y: m.py }; }));

var canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
canvas.style.cssText = 'width:100%;height:100%;display:block';
document.getElementById('container').appendChild(canvas);

function unit(chart) {
  var s = chart.scales && chart.scales.x;
  if (!s) return 0;
  return s.getPixelForValue(1) - s.getPixelForValue(0);
}
function fontSize(u) { return Math.max(6.5, Math.min(14, u * 0.36)); }

function arrowHead(ctx, x, y, dx, dy, s) {
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.moveTo(x, y);
  ctx.lineTo(x - dx * s - dy * s * 0.45, y - dy * s + dx * s * 0.45);
  ctx.lineTo(x - dx * s + dy * s * 0.45, y - dy * s - dx * s * 0.45);
  ctx.closePath();
  ctx.fill();
}

var SERIF = '"Times New Roman",Georgia,serif';
function drawTokens(ctx, toks, x, y, fs, align) {
  var widths = [], total = 0, k;
  for (k = 0; k < toks.length; k++) {
    var tk = toks[k], w;
    if (tk.t === 'x') { ctx.font = fs + 'px ' + SERIF; w = ctx.measureText(tk.s).width; }
    else {
      ctx.font = (fs * 0.85) + 'px ' + SERIF;
      w = Math.max(ctx.measureText(tk.n).width, ctx.measureText(tk.d).width) + fs * 0.26;
    }
    widths.push(w); total += w;
  }
  var cx = align === 'left' ? x : (align === 'right' ? x - total : x - total / 2);
  ctx.textBaseline = 'middle'; ctx.lineJoin = 'round'; ctx.lineCap = 'round';
  for (k = 0; k < toks.length; k++) {
    var tok = toks[k], ww = widths[k];
    if (tok.t === 'x') {
      ctx.font = fs + 'px ' + SERIF; ctx.textAlign = 'left';
